id: SceneVariants
name: ᝯㄝₓ Scene Variants
metadata:
  description: "A scene is often in your library twice: the whole thing, and a cut out of it. Stash has no way to say the two are the same work, so this plugin works it out and gives the scene page a tab that shows you.\n\nOpen any scene and there is a Variants tab beside Details, File Info and Edit. It lists every other scene sharing this one's stash-id, with the cover image and the preview loop it plays on hover, its resolution and running time, longest first - and where there is nothing to list it says why. The tab itself writes nothing: it is two read queries and a list of links. Hover a row and it tells you how that variant differs from the scene you are on: the tags it has that this one does not, the tags it is missing, and the names of the attributes that disagree - which fields differ, not what either of them says.\n\nName your full-length and partial-length tags below and each row says which it is, with the full-length ones sorted to the top - that is the question the tab exists to answer, and the tags are the cheap half of it. A name matches any of the tag's aliases too, and a scene tagged with anything under that tag in your hierarchy counts as tagged with it, so naming the general tag covers every specific one beneath it. Rows read Full-length and Partial-length rather than echoing your tag names back; the tag that decided a row is on its hover text. Leave both names empty and the tab still lists the variants, just without saying which is which.\n\nA stash-id, or the variant stash-id custom field this plugin's migration task writes, is the evidence used - a partial-length cut should not carry a real stash-id, because that id names the whole work, so the task moves it into a field of its own and the tab matches on both. A second task, Flag Variants, keeps a flag tag on every scene that has at least one other variant, so one tag filter lists every scene offering a choice - and putting that tag on scenes yourself makes them candidates the task offers to group into a variant set of their own, with a pseudo stash-id, even where no real one exists. A Synchronize Variants button on the tab pushes this scene's attribute values to its variants - every difference listed with a checkbox first, old value to new - the cover image too, if you turn that comparison on - tags, performers and groups only ever added so a variant's own extras survive - and a tag another tag on the same scene already implies is left out, which ᝯㄝₓ Normalize Parent Tags decides rather than this plugin - and a tag another tag on the same scene already implies is left out, which ᝯㄝₓ Normalize Parent Tags decides rather than this plugin - a group joined at this scene's own position in it, adds preselected while replaces stay unticked until you say so, and nothing written until you approve, with Undo while the dialog stays open. Saving a scene in its edit form offers the same dialog by itself, scoped to exactly the attributes that save changed, removals included, so a replaced URL or a dropped tag follows too - everything preselected except titles, since each line is the edit you just made - on by default and switchable off in the settings, with title changes only offered when a second setting opts them in. A third task, Review Variant Sets, works the other way round: it lists every multi-variant set in your library scored by how far its members have drifted apart - an attribute they disagree on is worth five by default, a tag, performer or group one carries and another does not is worth one, and all four numbers are editable in the dialog with a Remember option - worst first, so you can pick a set, pick the scene whose values are right, and push them to the rest one set at a time. A scene that never got one, or whose variants never got one, gets a tab that says so; matching on titles and on shared performers is not built yet, so about two scenes in three have nothing to list today. A scene carrying both tags at once is shown in red, because the two are meant to be exclusive.\n\nRequires Stash 0.28.0 or newer: the tab is added through the scene page's own plugin extension points, so there is no version of it that works without them."
